EXT. PIRATE'S COVE MINIGOLF - GAZEBO - DAY

A wedding in full swing on hole fourteen. Plastic palm trees, a fibreglass pirate ship, and forty folding chairs on AstroTurf. A string quartet plays on the putting green with a sad sag in the bow.

TAMSIN HOLT-MBEKI, 30s, bride, ivory gown, trainers underneath, pins her veil with the tip of a golf pencil. DEV RAMASWAMY, 30s, groom, tweed, holds a putter he hasn't realised he's holding. GRISHA ABRAMOV, 30s, best man, sweats through his waistcoat. MAGDALENA HOLT, 60s, mother of the bride, in a hat like a lifeboat, glares at a pirate statue.

A phone in Grisha's breast pocket buzzes. He fumbles it. Puts it on speaker.

FATHER OKONKWO (V.O.)
(tinny, wind in the line)
Grisha? It's Father Okonkwo.

GRISHA
Father! Where are you?

FATHER OKONKWO (V.O.)
Junction twelve. The motorway is a car park.

MAGDALENA
Tell him to walk.

FATHER OKONKWO (V.O.)
Who's that?

TAMSIN
It's Mum. Hi, Father.

FATHER OKONKWO (V.O.)
Tamsin! Lovely. Could you possibly begin without me?

DEV
(quietly)
I can't legally marry myself.

GRISHA
Father, we can't start without a priest.

FATHER OKONKWO (V.O.)
Yes you can. I'll do the vows by phone.

MAGDALENA
By phone!

TAMSIN
Mum.

MAGDALENA
In my day we had a priest in a collar, not a voice in a pocket!

DEV
Mrs. Holt, he's perfectly good in a pocket.

Grisha holds the phone aloft. The quartet falters. A recorded parrot in the pirate ship shrieks "Arr!" Magdalena claps a hand to her chest.

FATHER OKONKWO (V.O.)
Dearly beloved. Or whichever of you is nearest the phone.

TAMSIN
Go on, Father.

Dev takes her hand. Over the phone, a horn blares. Father Okonkwo shouts something that might be a blessing.
